```apex
public class AccountOwnershipService {

    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        TAF_Tx.disable(Account.sObjectType);
        try {
            List<Account> accounts = [SELECT Id, OwnerId FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds];
            List<Contact> contacts = [SELECT Id, OwnerId FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds];

            for (Account acc : accounts) {
                acc.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
            }
            update accounts;

            for (Contact con : contacts) {
                con.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
            }
            update contacts;
        } finally {
            TAF_Tx.enable(Account.sObjectType);
        }
    }
}
```

**How this meets every requirement:**

| Requirement | How it's satisfied |
|---|---|
| TAF bypass for Account | `TAF_Tx.disable(Account.sObjectType)` before the Account DML causes the `TAF_Handler` in the Account trigger to skip all registered actions for this transaction. |
| Contact actions run normally | Only `Account.sObjectType` is bypassed; Contact is unaffected. |
| Account automation restored after `reassign` | `TAF_Tx.enable(Account.sObjectType)` in the `finally` block runs whether the method succeeds or throws, restoring normal trigger behaviour for the remainder of the transaction. |
| All-or-none DML / exceptions propagate | There is no `catch` block. The `finally` only executes the enable call; any exception thrown by the DML statements propagates to the caller unchanged, rolling back both DML statements in the transaction. |
| ≤ 2 SOQL queries | One for Accounts, one for Contacts — constant regardless of volume. |
| ≤ 2 DML statements | One `update accounts`, one `update contacts`. |
| Bulk-safe up to 200 accounts | `WHERE Id IN :accountIds` and `WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds` handle the full set in a single query each. |

